{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792187375,"nonce":"WKbJsBZYhj06jxvBNkGngExMoo8=","title":"AnimatedValue.setTarget compares a new target with its first value, not the one it settled on","message":"`AnimatedValue.setTarget` starts a new spring when `target != current`, but `current` is only the initial value (or the last `setImmediate`). Once a spring settles and the scheduler drops it, setting the target it already holds starts a spring from that value to itself: the motion trace reports it as a one-frame spring \"started 17ms late\". Harmless to what is drawn, but noise in every motion report that springs a value back to where it is.\n\n**Reproducer**\n\nThe README banner's island (tools/snapshot/scenes/Island.hx) springs a panel's width and depth to an item's size each time it opens; reopening an item at the size it already has logs `spring #n value: 336 -\u003e 336`. Island's `Sprung.to` now skips that case itself.\n\n**Fix**\n\nOn settling, or in `setTarget`, compare against `get()` rather than `current`, and record the settled value in `current`.","files":null}]}